      [
        "Performance Features",
        "1. The novel impeller‑stator system with an innovative pulp circulation mode enables coarse minerals to be suspended in the middle zone of the cell at a relatively low impeller peripheral speed.\n2. It achieves flotation for both coarse and fine mineral particles. Coarse minerals undergo steady mineralization in the middle part of the cell, while the circulating pulp returning to the impeller features low concentration and fine particle size. This benefits flotation of both coarse and fine particles.\n3. A steady upward pulp flow is generated inside the cell, which facilitates the upward rise of mineralized froth attached with coarse minerals and greatly reduces the detachment rate between coarse minerals and bubbles.\n4. Low power consumption and low wear. Thanks to the low impeller peripheral speed and low concentration of circulating pulp returning to the impeller, the wear of impeller and stator is significantly reduced with low power consumption.\n5. Stable separation performance. The large clearance between impeller and stator ensures little change in aeration and air distribution even as impeller and stator wear, which provides favorable conditions for stable separation indicators.\n6. The grid plate forms a stable suspension layer for coarse particles. Its special structure reduces turbulence in the upper cell zone (flotation‑separation layer), greatly improving coarse‑particle flotation performance.\n7. Large aeration volume and stable liquid level, conducive to the upward floating of coarse particles.\n8. The flotation machine is available in suction‑feed cell and open‑flow cell types. Units can be arranged at the same elevation without froth pumps for middling return.\n9. It can treat coarse minerals with particle size up to 1 mm without sand‑settling at cell bottom.\n10. Upon customer request, an automatic control system for pulp level and aeration rate can be equipped for easy operation and adjustment.",
        "",
